// §4.6.4.2 / Syntax 4-4: `flicker_noise` takes TWO analog expressions. The
// exponent is not optional.
//
// Syntax 4-4, verbatim, in the LRM's own bracket notation where `[ ]` marks
// what may be omitted:
//
//     analog_small_signal_function_call ::=
//         ...
//       | white_noise ( analog_expression [ , string ] )
//       | flicker_noise ( analog_expression , analog_expression [ , string ] )
//
// The name is bracketed in both; the exponent is not. §4.6.4.2 states the same
// form in prose — "flicker_noise ( pwr , exp [ , name ] )" — and the exponent
// is load-bearing: "generates pink noise with a power of pwr at 1Hz which
// varies in proportion to 1/f exp". A one-argument call has no spectrum.
//
// WHY THIS IS A REFUSAL AND NOT A DEFAULT. There is no default in the clause,
// and inventing one is not conservative: `flicker_noise(kf)` read as exp = 1
// silently gives a 1/f source, while a model that meant `1/f^af` and lost the
// argument to a typo gets no diagnostic. §4.6.4.1's `white_noise(pwr)` is the
// one-argument member of the family and is how a flat spectrum is spelled, so
// nothing legal is lost by refusing this.
//
// VerA accepts it today — `lib/ir/lower.zig` seeds the exponent slot with
// `.f_one` and notes that "`flicker_noise(pwr[, exp[, name]])` defaults `exp`
// to 1" — and the generated device exports a `flicker` row with `.ef = 1`.
// Grammar, not folklore: the LRM's brackets say otherwise.
//
// THE `//! reject` PATTERN, corrected. An earlier draft wrote
// `//! reject flicker_noise` on the reasoning that no code is assigned yet, so
// the construct's name was all that could be demanded. That has no teeth:
// `failureContains` (tests/torture.zig:211-220) tests the pattern against
// `f.generated`, the generated Zig, before it looks at any diagnostic, and the
// generated Zig carries `flicker_noise` twice inside this module's own name.
// Any failure at all would have matched, including one with nothing to do with
// Syntax 4-4.
//
// `DiagnosticsReported` is the repo's convention for "a refusal is required and
// the code is not assigned yet" (48 files use it): it holds only if the failure
// carries at least one vera diagnostic. Today VerA emits none here — it accepts
// the call and seeds `exp` with 1 — so the fixture is red for its own reason.
// But any vera diagnostic at all satisfies it, so the tag is now the rule's own
// phrase, "flicker_noise requires an exponent", which the refusal's message has
// to carry. Replace it with the code once Syntax 4-4's arity rule is given one.
//
//! lrm 4.6.4.2
//! lrm A.8.2
//! reject flicker_noise requires an exponent
//! xfail VerA accepts the one-argument call and seeds the exponent with 1, exporting a flicker row with .ef = 1; Syntax 4-4 brackets only the name — `flicker_noise ( analog_expression , analog_expression [ , string ] )` — so the exponent is mandatory and there is no default to invent
module a06_flicker_noise_missing_exponent_rejected(p, n);
    inout p, n;
    electrical p, n;

    analog I(p, n) <+ V(p, n) / 1000.0 + flicker_noise(1e-20);
endmodule
